/*
 * purr_shell_exports.def - what the OS gives a loadable shell (the shell ABI, version 1; plan7.md "Shells as installable items").
 *
 * One line per name, in a fixed order: the position in this list is the number the shell is linked against (purrstrap gives each name the placeholder address
 * PURR_SHELL_IMPORT_BASE + 4 * position, the loader replaces every such address in the module with the real one from purr_shell_exports[]). So
 *   - a name is only ever ADDED at the end of its list (adding keeps ABI 1; removing or moving one is a new ABI number, PURR_SHELL_ABI_VERSION),
 *   - the file is included by C (purr_shell_abi.c builds the table, which fails to compile if a name does not exist) and read by purrstrap (scripts/shellabi.py).
 *
 *   SHELL_FN(name)             a function
 *   SHELL_DATA(type, name)     an object whose ADDRESS the shell uses (for example a font)
 *
 * The first list is what the Windows 10 Mobile shell (purr_w10m.c) imports today, measured on its compiled object (the undefined symbols); it is also the
 * set a third-party shell can rely on. Everything the shell needs that is not here is a compile or link error in the module build, not a crash on the device.
 */
/* C library and RTOS */
SHELL_FN(atoi)
SHELL_FN(calloc)
SHELL_FN(free)
SHELL_FN(localtime_r)
SHELL_FN(malloc)
SHELL_FN(memcpy)
SHELL_FN(memset)
SHELL_FN(pcTaskGetName)
SHELL_FN(snprintf)
SHELL_FN(strcmp)
SHELL_FN(strftime)
SHELL_FN(strlen)
SHELL_FN(time)
SHELL_FN(vTaskDelete)
SHELL_FN(xTaskCreatePinnedToCore)

